              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The **Lockheed P-3 Orion** is one of the most iconic and longest-serving aircraft in United States Navy history. First introduced in **1962**, this four-engine turboprop aircraft served as the Navy\u2019s premier land-based maritime patrol, reconnaissance, and anti-submarine warfare (ASW) platform for over six decades. \n\n### Current Service Status & Retirement\nThe U.S. Navy has spent the last decade phasing out the P-3 Orion in favor of its modern, jet-powered replacement, the **Boeing P-8A Poseidon**. \n\n* **Active-Duty Patrol Squadrons:** All active-duty Navy patrol squadrons (VP) completed their transition from the P-3C to the P-8A Poseidon by **May 2020**.\n* **Reserve Squadrons:** The Navy Reserve patrol squadrons completed their transition to the P-8A by **late 2023**.\n* **The \"Sundown\" of Manned Fleet Operations (2024\u20132025):** The last major operational variant of the P-3 in Navy service was the **EP-3E Aries II**, a highly specialized signals intelligence (SIGINT) aircraft. Operated by the \"World Watchers\" of Fleet Air Reconnaissance Squadron One (VQ-1) out of NAS Whidbey Island, the EP-3E completed its final operational deployment in October 2024. The Navy officially retired its last EP-3E on **February 12, 2025**, and VQ-1 was formally deactivated on **March 31, 2025**. \n* VQ-1's last standard P-3C (used for pilot training) was retired on **February 20, 2025**, marking the end of standard P-3 fleet operations.\n\n### Are Any P-3 Orions Still Flying?\nWhile the P-3 has been retired from frontline fleet service, a few Orions still fly in specialized capacities:\n\n1. **U.S. Navy Test & Evaluation:** A tiny handful of modified Orions (such as the NP-3D and specialized P-3Cs) are still operated for research, development, and testing by Air Test and Evaluation Squadron 30 (**VX-30**) at NAS Point Mugu, California, and Scientific Development Squadron One (**VXS-1**) at NAS Patuxent River, Maryland.\n2. **NOAA \"Hurricane Hunters\":** The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) continues to operate two heavily modified **WP-3D Orions** (affectionately named *\"Kermit\"* and *\"Miss Piggy\"*) to fly directly into hurricanes to gather meteorological data.\n3. **Foreign Militaries:** Several international allies continue to fly the P-3 Orion, including Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, Germany, and Greece. In addition, Argentina acquired several surplus U.S. Navy P-3Cs to upgrade its maritime patrol capabilities.\n\n---\n\n### Key Features of the P-3 Orion\n* **The \"MAD\" Boom:** The most distinctive feature of the P-3 is the long fiberglass stinger extending from the tail. This houses the **Magnetic Anomaly Detector (MAD)**, which detects the metallic hulls of submerged submarines by measuring disruptions in Earth's magnetic field.\n* **Engine Power:** It is powered by four Allison T56-A-14 turboprop engines, which allow the plane to loiter at low altitudes and slow speeds over the ocean for up to 10 to 12 hours. To conserve fuel during long patrols, crews often shut down one of the engines while on station.\n* **Weapons Bay and Payload:** Despite being a patrol plane, the P-3C has a bomb bay and wing pylons capable of carrying an extensive arsenal, including Mk 46/50/54 torpedoes, Harpoon anti-ship missiles, SLAM-ER missiles, mines, and depth charges. It also deploys sonobuoys through tubes in the underbelly to acoustically track submarines.",
